direction: down

title: |md
  ## epoll vs io_uring for sockets
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

epoll: "epoll (legacy, well-known)\n\nepoll_create1(0) → epfd\nepoll_ctl(epfd, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, sock_fd, ...)\nepoll_wait(epfd, events, max, timeout)\n\nEdge-triggered or level-triggered.\nO(1) registration; O(events) wait.\n\nEPOLLEXCLUSIVE — multiple workers,\none wakes per event (no thundering herd)." {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
}

iouring: "io_uring (modern)\n\nSubmit recv/send operations as SQEs.\nKernel reports completions via CQEs.\n\nNet-specific ops (since 5.x):\n- IORING_OP_RECV / SEND\n- IORING_OP_RECVMSG / SENDMSG\n- IORING_OP_ACCEPT\n- IORING_OP_CONNECT\n- IORING_OP_RECVMSG_MULTISHOT (5.18+)\n\nZero-copy variants:\n- IORING_OP_SEND_ZC\n- IORING_OP_SENDMSG_ZC\n\nHigher throughput, more complex." {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
}

verdict: "When to pick\n\nepoll: 80% of cases. Mature, simple, performant.\nio_uring: highest throughput, batch-y workloads,\nif you can pay the complexity tax." {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
}

epoll -> iouring -> verdict
